WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.160
Two tiny arterioles set the kidney filter's pressure and its flow.

2
00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:07.680
But the arterial pressure feeding them changes.

3
00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:10.940
How does filtration stay nearly steady?

4
00:00:14.483 --> 00:00:18.883
In mammals, kidney blood flow and filtration stay nearly constant.

5
00:00:20.043 --> 00:00:25.663
That holds over perfusion pressures of about 80 to 180 mmHg.

6
00:00:27.063 --> 00:00:29.543
On a graph, it is a long, nearly flat stretch.

7
00:00:30.863 --> 00:00:32.183
This is autoregulation.

8
00:00:33.523 --> 00:00:38.423
Two mechanisms do most of it, both acting mainly on the afferent arteriole.

9
00:00:39.217 --> 00:00:40.677
The first reads stretch.

10
00:00:41.977 --> 00:00:45.997
When rising pressure stretches the arteriole's smooth muscle, the muscle

11
00:00:45.997 --> 00:00:50.657
depolarizes. Calcium inside it rises, and it contracts.

12
00:00:52.017 --> 00:00:57.137
This is the myogenic response, which Bayliss described in arteries in 1902.

13
00:00:58.597 --> 00:01:00.697
What senses the stretch is still debated.

14
00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:03.980
The second reads the tubule instead.

15
00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:08.260
Each tubule comes back to touch its own glomerulus.

16
00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:13.980
There, at the end of the thick ascending limb, sits a plaque of cells, the macula

17
00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:18.900
densa. So the fluid leaving the loop can adjust filtration upstream.

18
00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:25.710
More salt (NaCl) arriving there constricts the afferent arteriole.

19
00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:31.550
Less salt dilates it, and renin-secreting cells in the arteriole wall release renin.

20
00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:34.810
This is tubuloglomerular feedback.

21
00:01:36.033 --> 00:01:41.113
Salt enters the macula densa cells through a carrier called NKCC2.

22
00:01:42.313 --> 00:01:45.053
When more arrives, the cells release ATP.

23
00:01:45.053 --> 00:01:48.753
Adenosine made from it acts on A1 receptors.

24
00:01:49.953 --> 00:01:54.313
The afferent arteriole constricts, so the filter's pressure and filtration fall.

25
00:01:55.367 --> 00:01:56.867
Here is the one idea.

26
00:01:58.147 --> 00:02:01.347
Two signals, stretch and salt, do most of the steadying.

27
00:02:02.627 --> 00:02:05.787
Stretch is read in the arteriole wall, salt in the tubule.

28
00:02:07.067 --> 00:02:11.567
Both act mainly on one valve upstream of the filter, the afferent arteriole.

29
00:02:12.617 --> 00:02:15.117
Diabetes puts the salt loop to a test.

30
00:02:16.317 --> 00:02:19.097
High plasma glucose raises filtered glucose.

31
00:02:20.377 --> 00:02:24.977
The proximal tubule takes back more of it, with sodium, through a carrier called

32
00:02:24.977 --> 00:02:29.877
SGLT2. So less salt reaches the macula densa.

33
00:02:31.017 --> 00:02:32.317
What happens to filtration?

34
00:02:38.383 --> 00:02:44.023
It rises. With less salt arriving, the afferent arteriole dilates.

35
00:02:45.323 --> 00:02:48.203
Pressure in the filter climbs, and filtration with it.

36
00:02:49.383 --> 00:02:50.583
This is hyperfiltration.

37
00:02:52.103 --> 00:02:56.583
Whether blunted feedback or other upstream dilation dominates is still open.

38
00:02:57.633 --> 00:03:00.513
So the filter is steadied mostly at its inlet.

39
00:03:01.673 --> 00:03:05.733
The arteriole wall reads the pressure, and the tubule reads the salt.

40
00:03:07.053 --> 00:03:11.813
Both answers come back to the same upstream valve, across that wide range of

41
00:03:11.813 --> 00:03:12.133
pressures.
